COMMENT
Static web site generator for music, video and book libraries
=============================================================

This program takes a tree of files and creates a set of HTML files that
contain links to navigate in the same way as a file tree can be navigated.

It is a sort of specialised static web site generator.
It has so far only been tested with the NGINX server on Debian Linux.

Top level directories in the tree get links in an index.html.
Each sub-directory gets its own HTML file with links to either:
 - other (lower level) sub-directories 
 - terminal HTML files.

The terminal HTML file contains what is needed to present the contents
of the terminal (non-directory) file.

The terminal files may be large and the file tree may be on a remote
machine, mounted by SMB or NFS. This poses a problem for web servers,
which, by default, only serve files from their own HTML tree
(e.g. /var/www/html/...). For example, an SMB share on another machine
on the LAN may be mounted at /mnt/videolib. This is the file tree to
be converted to HTML links, but the terminal files must be referenced
with a root explicity declared to the web server as an alias. As an example, this
addition to an NGINX configuration file makes /mnt/videolib/... capable of
being referenced as /external-files/...

/etc/nginx/sites-available/default
----------------------------------
	root /var/www/html;

	location /external-files/ {
		 alias /mnt/videolib/;
	}

To accomodate this security dance, the terminal file name must have its
true root (here /mnt/videolib) replaced by /external-files (one of the
tasks this program performs).

This was originally developed to create a version of a local video
library than can be accessed from any modern browser on the LAN. The
default terminal file contents reflect this application.

Processing for a local audio/music library was quickly added, once
accessing the video library was found to work.

Processing for e-books has also ben added.

It is possible to specify any program or shell script file to be
run to generate the contents of a terminal HTML file, though, so
this is a somewhat general purpose application. That program is
passed the terminal HTML file name and the tree file name (with modified root)
and should generate the required HTML contents to display (etc.) the
tree file.

Examples
--------

To generate HTML pages for a video library (minimal example):
a68g files_to_html.a68 -- -o tx -v

This builds the web pages for a video collection in a directory called tx using all defaults
apart from some verbose output to monitor progress.

Another version of this:
a68g files_to_html.a68 -- -o tx -v -p video -r /mnt/videolib -f external-files 
   -i vindex.html -t "Film and TV Collection"

To generate HTML pages for an audio library:
a68g files_to_html.a68 -- -r /mnt/audiolib/ -o audexp2 -p audio -v -e '.flac,.mp3' --multi
   -f external-files2 -i aindex.html -a -t "Music Collection"

This builds the web pages for an audio collection in a directory called audexp2. The terminal
file processing is set to the internal audio path. The file extensions to consider are .flac
and .mp3 in a file tree with a root at /mnt/audiolib. The terminal audio files are to have links in
the final directory level rather than individual terminal HTML files being created for each
terminal file. This is useful for audio files, where many players can sensibly be stacked on a
single page. For video files, it is best if each video file has its own HTML file as the
players are large. The desired top level index file name is specified (so it doesn't overwrite
the default index.html). Alphabetic sub-indexes are requested. The index file then contains
only A, B, C ... as links to sub-index files which contain links to the top directory level
in the file hierarchy. This is useful for audio, where these top level directories are
usually "artist names" (in the structure used with mpd, for example). As there may be
many artists in a collection, this keeps the top level index (and sub-indices) manageably short.
The external files alias used by the web server for the audio files is to be external-files2.

To generate HTML pages for a book library:
a68g files_to_html.a68 -- -r /mnt/booklib/ -o bookexp -p book -v -e '.pdf' --multi
   -f external-files3 -i bindex.html -t "Book Collection"

This is very similar to the audio library case. The "book" built-in processing just creates
links to the PDF files, though -- no 'player' is needed. It is assumed the browser can find
a suitable way of displaying PDF files itself.

Scripts
-------

mountaudio.sh - Example of mounting an audio library from an SMB share.
mountvideo.sh - Example of mounting a video library from an SMB share.
mountbooks.sh - Example of mounting a book library from an SMB share.
map-video-files.sh - Example of how to organise (mostly) flat files using symbolic links for files_to_html.a68.
fix-720-aspect.sh - Make 576x720 16:9 video appear with the correct aspect ratio in an HTML 5 video player.

Usage notes
-----------

Once a directory full of HTML files has been created by this program, simply copy the entire
contents to the web server HTML tree, For example:

cd bookexp
sudo cp * /var/www/html/

The 'external-files...' referenced with '-f' options must be set up as aliases in the webserver
configuration file, as explained above.  After modifying the configuration files, the webserver
must be restarted. For example:

sudo systemctl restart nginx

If 'external-files' refers to an SMB share mounted on the webserver machine, the SMB server
machine must (obviously) be configured with that share. Some examples:

[videolib]
  comment = Movie and video library.
  path = /massbackup/contents/videolib
  available = yes
  read only = no
  browsable = yes
  public = yes
  writeable = yes
  follow symlinks = yes
  wide links = yes

[booklib]
  comment = Book library.
  path = /massbackup/contents/newbooks
  available = yes
  read only = no
  browsable = yes
  public = yes
  writeable = yes
  follow symlinks = yes
  wide links = yes

Note that for writeable access to actually work, the entire path must have write permissions
(on every part of the path). Note also that giving the user used to 'login' to the SMB server
an SMB specific password (rather than use their system password) is a good idea. Use 'smbpasswd'
to to this (set it to something trivial like 12345678). This password can reasonably be used
in mount scripts.

Nick Glazzard 2026
------------------
COMMENT

PR heap=256M PR # Probably not needed, actually. #

PR read "utility.a68" PR
PR read "dictionaries.a68" PR
PR read "arg_parser.a68" PR

# Generate HTML files which use links to mimic the arrangement of a tree of files. #
# The root of the file tree to convert to links is file_tree_root. This becomes an index file. #
# The exts argument is a comma separated link of terminal file extensions to notice. ".mp4,.mkv" etc. #
# The output directory for HTML files (created if it doesn't exist, emptied if it does) is uhtml_output. #
# trm_pgm is the name of a script file (etc.) to call to produce the terminal file contents. #
# If it is an empty string, video or audio, use an appropriate built-in production process. #
# index is the name of an index file to put top level links in. #
# files is the name of an external files alias for NGINX. #
# title will be added to the index page if it isn't empty. #
# If multi, put multiple terminal files in the last directory mimic HTML file. #
# Otherwise, put a single terminal file link in a mimic HTML file for that terminal file. #
# If verbose, issue some progress information. #
# Return: 0: OK; 1, 2: Failed to empty output dir;  3-8: Append to files failed. #
# 9: Expected file did not exist (logic error). #
PROC file_tree_to_html_links = (STRING file_tree_root, exts, uhtml_output, trm_pgm, index, files, title,
                                BOOL multi, alpha_tabs, verbose) INT:
(
   INT retval := 0;
   INT ec := 0;      # Error count. #
   CHAR k_ds = "@";  # Directory separator for mimic file names. Was +. @ has fewer conflicts. #
   INT html_file_count := 0;
   STRING html_output := uhtml_output;

   # Empty the HTML file output directory. #
   (NOT run_command("rm -rf " + uhtml_output) | retval := 1; ec +:= 1; done);
   (NOT run_command("mkdir " + uhtml_output) | retval := 2; ec +:= 1; done);

   # Generate an HTML page header. #
   PROC make_html_header = (STRING title) []STRING:
   (
      REF STRING_LIST lines := STRING_LIST_INIT LOC STRING_LIST;
      lines +:= "<!DOCTYPE html>";
      lines +:= "<html lang=""en"">";
      lines +:= "<head>";
      lines +:= "<meta charset=""UTF-8"">";
      lines +:= "<meta name=""viewport"" content=""width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"">";
      lines +:= "<title>" + title + "</title>";
      lines +:= "</head>";
      lines +:= "<body>";
      string_list_as_array(lines)
   );

   # Create files that mimic a directory tree, containing hypertext links that  #
   # allow that tree to be navigated. The s string is a file name in the directory tree. #
   PROC make_html_files = (STRING s) BOOL:
   (
      # Look at the file name after the tree root. #
      STRING after := s[(ELEMS root)+1:];
      IF after /= ""
      THEN
         # Convert / in the name to +. The output files are to be in a single directory. #
         STRING after_name := replace_char(after, "/", k_ds, TRUE);
         STRING after_output := html_output+"/"+after_name+".html"; # Output file name. #

         # If the tree file is a directory and its mimic file doesn't exist yet, create the mimic. #
         IF file is directory(s)
         THEN
            IF NOT file is regular(after_output)
            THEN
               (verbose | print(("DIR: ", after_output, new line)));

               # Create an HTML file to put links to sub-directories in. #
               (append_lines(make_html_header(replace_char(after_name,k_ds," ",TRUE)), after_output) /= 0 |
                     ec +:= 1; retval := 3); # HTML leader. #
               (append_lines(("<h1>"+replace_char(after_name,k_ds," ",TRUE)+"</h1>"), after_output) /= 0 |
                     ec +:= 1; retval := 4); # Title. #
               html_file_count +:= 1;

               # Find items needed to construct a link. #
               STRING href_string := "/" + after_name + ".html";
               STRINGPAIR stem_term := splitlast(after_name, k_ds);
               STRING presented_name := last OF stem_term;

               # If the mimic file for the parent directory exists, put a link to this directory #
               # mimic file in it. #
               STRINGPAIR parent := splitlast(after_output, k_ds);
               IF file is regular(first OF parent + ".html")
               THEN
                  STRING link := "<a href=""" + href_string + """>" + presented_name + "</a><br>";
                  (append_lines((link), first OF parent + ".html") /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 5)

               # If the directory is at the top level, a mimic file will not exist. Otherwise, it must. #
               ELSE
                  IF count_char_occurrences(k_ds, after_output) > 1
                  THEN
                     put(stand error, 
                         ("make_html_files(): ERROR: Parent directory mimic file does not exist for ",s,new line));
                     ec +:= 1; retval := 9
                            
                  # At the top level, a link needs to be added to an index.html file. #
                  ELSE
                     STRING link := "<a href=""" + href_string + """>" + 
                                    replace_string(first OF stem_term, k_ds, "", TRUE) + "</a><br>";

                     # If creating sub-index files for each unique top level file name first character ... #
                     IF alpha_tabs
                     THEN
                        STRING leading_alpha := (first OF stem_term)[2];
                        STRING alpha_index_name := "/" + leading_alpha + "_" + index;
                        STRING alpha_index_file := html_output + alpha_index_name;
                        STRING alpha_link := "<a href=""" + alpha_index_name + """>" + leading_alpha + "</a><br>";

                        # If the sub-index for this first character doesn't exist, create it. #
                        # Put a link to the new sub-index file in the top level index file. #
                        IF NOT file is regular(alpha_index_file)
                        THEN
                           (append_lines((make_html_header(leading_alpha + " Sub-index")), alpha_index_file) /= 0 | 
                                 ec +:= 1; retval := 4);
                           (append_lines((alpha_link), html_output + "/"+index) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 5)
                        FI;

                        # Always put a link to the top level file in the sub-index. #
                        (append_lines((link), alpha_index_file) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 5)

                     # Not using first character sub-index files. #
                     ELSE
                        (append_lines((link), html_output + "/"+index) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 5)
                     FI
                  FI
               FI
            FI

         # Tree file is not a directory, but a terminal file. #
         ELSE
            STRINGPAIR parent := splitlast(after_output, k_ds);
            STRING parent_name := first OF parent + ".html";

            # Verify the HTML mimic file for the directory exists at this point. #
            IF file is regular(parent_name)
            THEN
               STRINGPAIR name_ext := splitext(after_name);
               STRING new_terminal := replace_string(after_name, last OF name_ext, "", TRUE);
               STRINGPAIR stem_term := splitlast(new_terminal, k_ds);
               STRING presented_name := last OF stem_term;
               STRING external_files_s := replace_string(s, file_tree_root, files, FALSE);
               STRING new_terminal_output := "";

               # Put links to actual terminal files in the last directory file if multi. #
               IF multi
               THEN
                  STRING link := "<a href=""" + external_files_s + """>" + presented_name + "</a><br>";
                  (append_lines((link), parent_name) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 6);
                  new_terminal_output := parent_name;
                  (verbose | print(("LNK: ", link[:min2int(ELEMS link,50)], "...", new line)))

               # Otherwise, make an HTML file and put a link to the terminal file in it. #
               # Put a link to the newly made file in the parent directory mimic file.#
               ELSE
                  # Link to new file in parent directory mimic. #
                  STRING href_string := "/" + new_terminal + ".html";
                  STRING link := "<a href=""" + href_string + """>" + presented_name + "</a><br>";
                  (append_lines((link), parent_name) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 6);

                  # Make new terminal file mimic. Link to terminal tree file in terminal mimic. #
                  new_terminal_output := html_output + "/" + new_terminal + ".html";
                  html_file_count +:= 1;
                  (verbose | print(("TRM: ", new_terminal_output, new line)))
               FI; # Multiple terminal file links in parent file or not.  #

               # Video file processing if no trm_pgm is supplied, or it is the keyword "video". #
               IF trm_pgm = "" OR trm_pgm = "video"
               THEN
                  STRING videofile_ext := last OF splitext(s);
                  (NOT multi |
                        (append_lines(
                              make_html_header(replace_string(replace_char(after_name,k_ds," ",TRUE),videofile_ext," ",TRUE)), 
                              new_terminal_output) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 7)
                  );
                  STRING videotype := videofile_ext[2:];
                  (append_lines(("<video width=""640"" height=""360"" controls>",
                                 "  <source src="""+external_files_s+""" type=""video/"+videotype+""">",
                                 "  Your browser does not support the video tag",
                                 "</video>"), new_terminal_output) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 8);
                  (append_lines(("<br><hr><br>"), new_terminal_output) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 8)

               # Audio file processing. #
               ELIF trm_pgm = "audio"
               THEN
                  STRING audiofile_ext := last OF splitext(s);
                  (NOT multi |
                        (append_lines(
                              make_html_header(replace_string(replace_char(after_name,k_ds," ",TRUE),audiofile_ext," ",TRUE)), 
                              new_terminal_output) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 7)
                  );
                  STRING audiotype := audiofile_ext[2:];
                  (append_lines(("<audio controls>",
                                 "  <source src="""+external_files_s+""" type=""audio/"+audiotype+""">",
                                 "  Your browser does not support the audio tag",
                                 "</audio>"), new_terminal_output) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 8);
                  (append_lines(("<br><hr><br>"), new_terminal_output) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 8)

               # Book file processing. #
               ELIF trm_pgm = "book"
               THEN
                  STRING bookfile_ext := last OF splitext(s);
                  (NOT multi |
                        (append_lines(
                              make_html_header(replace_string(replace_char(after_name,k_ds," ",TRUE),bookfile_ext," ",TRUE)), 
                              new_terminal_output) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 7);
                        STRING link := "<a href=""" + external_files_s + """>" + presented_name + "</a><br>";
                        (append_lines((link), new_terminal_output) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 6);
                        (append_lines(("<br><hr><br>"), new_terminal_output) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 8);
                  )

               # Or run a specified program. #
               ELSE
                  STRING pgm_cmd := trm_pgm + " '" + new_terminal_output + "' '" + external_files_s + "'";
                  CO print(("*** ", pgm_cmd, new line)); CO
                  (NOT run_command(pgm_cmd) | 
                        put(stand error, ("make_html_files(): ERROR: Cannot run specified terminal program.",
                                          new line)))
               FI # Program to generate terminal file contents. #

            ELSE
               put(stand error, 
                   ("make_html_files(): ERROR: Expected parent file not found for terminal file: ",
                    first OF parent,new line));
               ec +:= 1; retval := 9
            FI # Parent mimic file exists. #
         FI # Not directory. #
      FI; # File name - root name not empty. #
      FALSE # Keep going. #
   );

   # Try to sort audio file names correctly even though some file names have track numbers #
   # without leading zeros. E.g. 1 instead of 01, etc. Sorting these in the default #
   # way gives tracks 1, then 10, then 2 ... so album tracks are out of order.  #
   PROC audio_sort = ([]STRING names) []STRING:
   (
      INT n := ELEMS names;
      [n]STRING modified_names;
      [n]STRING sorted_names;
      FOR i TO n
      DO 
         # Split the file name into space separated words. #
         FLEX [1:0]STRING words := split_string(names[i], " ");
         FOR j TO ELEMS words
         DO
            # A word comprising decimal digits should be converted to nnn with leading zeros. #
            (all_digits(words[j]) | 
                  STRING mod_int;
                  INT value := string_to_int(words[j]);
                  putsf(mod_int, ($3d$, clamp_int(value,0,999)));
                  words[j] := mod_int
            )
         OD;
         # Assemble the possibly modified words to a string again. #
         modified_names[i] := join_string(words, " ")
         CO print((modified_names[i], new line)) CO
      OD;
      # Sort the modified names with an index sort. #
      FLEX [1:0]INT indices := isort_strings(modified_names);
      # Return the original file names sorted according to the modified names. #
      FOR i TO n DO sorted_names[i] := names[indices[i]] OD;
      sorted_names
   );

   # Add trailer text to HTML files. This is a callback for all_files(). #
   PROC finish_html_files = (STRING s) BOOL:
   (
      # Treat audio processing with multi specially by generating a player #
      # with Javascript to play all files in a terminal directory. #
      IF trm_pgm = "audio" AND multi
      THEN
         audio_multi_play_all(s)
      FI;

      # End the HTML page. #
      (append_lines(("<br><hr><a href=""/" + index + """>Top</a><br>",
                     "Last updated: " + date_time_string(FALSE) +"<br><hr>",
                     "</body>",
                     "</html>"), s) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 9);
      FALSE # Continue processing files. #
   );

   # Output Javascript and other elements to "play all" files in a terminal HTML directory mimic. #
   # This is only relevant to audio processing with the multi option. #
   PROC audio_multi_play_all = (STRING s) VOID:
   (
      # Read the HTML file as it currently is. Look for audio files in it. #
      REF STRING_LIST src_files := STRING_LIST_INIT LOC STRING_LIST;
      FILE fin;
      (open_file(s, "r+", fin) /= 0 |
            put(stand error, ("audio_multi_play_all(): Failed to open HTML file: ", s, new line));
            stop
      );

      BOOL eof := FALSE;
      PROC at_eof = (REF FILE afin) BOOL: ( eof := TRUE );
      STRING input_line;
      on file end(fin, at_eof);

      DO
         get(fin, (input_line, new line)); (eof | file_end);
         INT start, end;
         IF grep in string("src=", input_line, start, end) = 0
         THEN
            IF grep in string("external-files2.*\.flac", input_line, start, end) = 0
            THEN
               src_files +:= input_line[start:end]
            ELIF grep in string("external-files2.*\.mp3", input_line, start, end) = 0
            THEN
               src_files +:= input_line[start:end]
            FI
         FI
      OD;

      file_end:
      close(fin);

      # If we found more than one audio file, create another audio player for the whole lot. #
      IF ELEMS src_files > 1
      THEN
         []STRING player_all = \
          ("<h2>Play All</h2>",
           "<p>Current track: <span id=""track-counter""></span></p>",
           "<audio id =""audio-player"" controls>",
              "Your browser does not support the audio element.",
           "</audio>");
         (append_lines(player_all, s) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 6);

         # Write Javascript to make the "all tracks" player work. #
         REF STRING_LIST java_script := STRING_LIST_INIT LOC STRING_LIST;
         FLEX [1:0]STRING src_files_array := string_list_as_array(src_files);
         INT n := ELEMS src_files_array;
         java_script +:= "<script>";
         java_script +:= "   const playlist = [";
         FOR i TO n
         DO
            java_script +:= "      """+src_files_array[i]+""""+(i < n | "," | "")
         OD;
         java_script +:= "   ];";

         java_script +:= "   let currentTrackIndex = 0;";
         java_script +:= "   document.getElementById(""track-counter"").textContent = currentTrackIndex + 1;";
         java_script +:= "   const player = document.getElementById('audio-player');";
         java_script +:= "   player.src = playlist[currentTrackIndex];";
         java_script +:= "   player.addEventListener('ended', function() {";
         java_script +:= "      currentTrackIndex++;";
         java_script +:= "      if (currentTrackIndex < playlist.length) {";
         java_script +:= "          document.getElementById(""track-counter"").textContent = currentTrackIndex + 1;";
         java_script +:= "          player.src = playlist[currentTrackIndex];";
         java_script +:= "          player.play();";
         java_script +:=  "      } else {";
         java_script +:= "          console.log(""Playlist finished."");";
         java_script +:=  "      }";
         java_script +:=  "   });";
         java_script +:= "</script>";
         (append_lines(string_list_as_array(java_script), s) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 6)
      FI # More than one audio source file in a terminal directory mimic. #
   );

   # Start an index.html file. #
   STRING index_name := html_output + "/" + index;
   STRING final_title := (title = "" | "Index" | title + " Index");
   (append_lines((make_html_header(final_title)), index_name) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 4; done);
   (append_lines(("<h1>"+final_title+"</h1>"), index_name) /= 0 | ec +:= 1; retval := 4);

   # Process the input tree, making HTML files.  #
   (verbose | print(("Processing the input tree ...", new line)));
   STRING_SORT_PROC sorter := (trm_pgm = "audio" | audio_sort | default_string_sort);
   all_files_ex(root, exts, make_html_files, FALSE, TRUE, sorter);

   # Process the output directory, completing the HTML files. #
   (verbose | print(("Completing output HTML files ...", new line)));
   all_files(html_output, ".html", finish_html_files, FALSE, TRUE);

   # Final statistics if desired. #
   done:
   (verbose | print(("Generated ", wint(html_file_count), 
                     " HTML files. Return code = ", wint(retval), 
                     " Error count = ", wint(ec), new line)));

   retval
);


# ------------ #
# MAIN PROGRAM #
# ------------ #

REF ARG_PARSE arg_parser := make_arg_parser("Convert a tree of files to HTML files and links");
arg_set_description(arg_parser,
"This program takes a tree of files and creates a set of HTML files that\n\
contain links to navigate in the same way as the file tree can be navigated.\n\
The final links are to terminal HTML files that display or process the\n\
non-directory files in the original file tree.\n\
This was written to allow a local video library to be navigated and the\n\
videos in it played in a modern browser using HTML 5 capabilities.\n\
It can process local audio libraries too.\n\
It may have other applications, though.");
REF STRING root := arg_add_string_option(arg_parser, "-r", "--root", 
                                         "File tree root directory.", "/mnt/videolib");
REF STRING exts := arg_add_string_option(arg_parser, "-e", "--exts", 
                                         "File extensions to notice.", ".mp4");
REF STRING outdir := arg_add_string_option(arg_parser, "-o", "--outdir",
                                           "Output directory for HTML files (EMPTIED!)", "./tree-output");
REF STRING trm_pgm := arg_add_string_option(arg_parser, "-p", "--program", 
                                            "Program to generate terminal HTML file contents", "");
REF STRING index := arg_add_string_option(arg_parser, "-i", "--index", 
                                          "Name of index file to generate", "index.html");
REF STRING files := arg_add_string_option(arg_parser, "-f", "--files", 
                                          "External files alias (see NGINX docs)", "external-files");
REF STRING title := arg_add_string_option(arg_parser, "-t", "--title", 
                                          "Title for index page.", "");
REF BOOL multi := arg_add_flag(arg_parser, "-m", "--multi", 
                               "Put terminal file links for a directory in a single file.", FALSE);
REF BOOL alpha := arg_add_flag(arg_parser, "-a", "--alpha", 
                               "Create alphabetic sub-indices.", FALSE);
REF BOOL verbose := arg_add_flag(arg_parser, "-v", "--verbose", 
                                 "Output informational messages.", FALSE);
( NOT parse_args(arg_parser) | 
      put(stand error, ("Unknown or malformed command line arguments.", newline)); stop );

print(("Status = ", wint(file_tree_to_html_links(root,     # file tree root #
                                                 exts,     # file extensions to notice #
                                                 outdir,   # output directory fot HTML files #
                                                 trm_pgm,  # program to run to generate terminal files #
                                                 index,    # index (top level) HTML file to generate #
                                                 files,    # external files alias to use #
                                                 title,    # index page title #
                                                 multi,    # put links to files in dir HTML not single terminal files #
                                                 alpha,    # create alphabetic sub-indices #
                                                 verbose)), new line))
